  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery and RAP height analysis place a \nshortwave trough over eastern Upper MI with a ridge building \nupstream over the Dakotas and MN. At the surface, high pressure is \nbuilding in from the northwest. Delta-Ts across Lake Superior of 12-\n15 C are supporting lake effect rain and snow showers noted on \nradar. Snow is mainly occuring above 1000 ft in elevation in the \nwest and central UP. Ground temps in the mid 30s to low 40s prevent \nany snow from accumulating on paved surfaces/roadways, confirmed \nwith available webcams, leaving no travel impacts for the morning \ncommute. Any accumulations should be a dusting to 1 inch mainly on \ngrass or elevated surfaces, save for up to 2-3 inches in the \nMichigamme Highlands. Temps settle into the upper 20s to upper 30s \nthis morning. Showers dissipate from west to east today due to a \nwarming and drying airmass. Brisk winds follow a similar pattern, \ntapering down from west to east today; still expecting gusts up to \naround 35 mph near eastern Lake Superior this morning. Temps warm \ninto the upper 30s to mid 40s under mostly cloudy skies.\n\nWith high pressure overhead tonight, calm conditions and colder \ntemps are expected. Where clearing occurs much earlier in the west, \ntemps fall into the upper teens to mid 20s. Lows near the Great \nLakes shores will be in the upper 20s to low 30s. Lake effect cloud \ncover insulates into the early overnight hours in the east, keeping \nlows moreso in the mid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nA Pacific jet allows for more mild air across the CONUS and southern \nCanada, this weekend into early next week. Highs trend to the upper \n40s to 50s by Monday with lows in the upper 20s to 30s. A trough \ndigging southeast across Ontario on Sunday sends a sfc low over far \nnorthern Ontario. A cold front turns winds westerly for Sunday, \nbecoming gusty by the afternoon. Frequent gusts of 20-25 mph are \nexpected in the south half and 25-35 mph in the north. A brief hit \nof lake enhanced to lake effect rain showers are possible with this \nsystem, highest confidence over the east (20-50% chance) Sunday \nafternoon. Early next week brings a clipper system before high \npressure returns for much of the remainder of the work week. 850 mb \ntemps fall to at least -7C by Wednesday, remaining steadily cool out \nof the north. This would favor some periods of lake effect showers \nas well as closer to normal temps (highs in the upper 30s to mid \n40s and lows in the 20s to low 30s) through late next week.",
